    "findings": [
      {
        "id": "F01",
        "claim": "Deploy dep-8841 (09:14:00\u201309:14:47, all 6 instances) changed only payment_client.py: added 503 to status_forcelist, allowed_methods {GET}->None (so POST /v1/charges became retryable), backoff 0.5->0, total=3 (up to 4 attempts). The runbook says the DB connection is held for the whole handle_checkout(), including every payment retry, and the pool is 15 per instance with pool_timeout=10s.",
        "source_ids": [
          "S01"
        ],
        "alternative": "none"
      },
      {
        "id": "F02",
        "claim": "The deploy alone caused no errors. From 09:15:00 to 09:19:30, 200 of the sampled traces (969 total) returned 200 with max duration 229ms. The change stayed latent until the gateway started returning 503s.",
        "source_ids": [
          "S19"
        ],
        "alternative": "none"
      },
      {
        "id": "F03",
        "claim": "TRIGGER: the third-party payment gateway returned 'HTTP 503 (upstream capacity)' on POST /v1/charges from 09:19:37.951 to 09:21:09.037 (138 errors, ~91s). Its 5xx rate peaked at ~87% at 09:21:00 and was 0 by 09:21:45.",
        "source_ids": [
          "S16",
          "S07",
          "S03"
        ],
        "alternative": "none"
      },
      {
        "id": "F04",
        "claim": "The new client retried POST charges immediately, with no backoff, while still holding the DB connection. Example: trace req-01826 made 4 charge attempts (503 at 7073ms, 8451ms, 11370ms, then 200 at 196ms), total 27119ms. Logs show 34 checkouts that 'completed after retries' with conn_hold of 7\u201328s, and 10 that failed with payment_gateway_unavailable after 4 attempts and ~31s of connection hold. Retrying a non-idempotent POST charge also creates a double-charge risk.",
        "source_ids": [
          "S14",
          "S18",
          "S13"
        ],
        "alternative": "none"
      },
      {
        "id": "F05",
        "claim": "The DB pool went from 1 to 14 at 09:19:45 and stayed at capacity 15 from 09:20:00 to ~09:21:05, then dropped back to 1. There were 105 app-originated 503s with db_pool_checkout_timeout (waited_ms=10000, pool_in_use=15/15) from 09:19:56.7 to 09:20:59.5: 42 GET /orders/{id}, 26 GET /cart, 37 POST /checkout. GET /cart and /orders never call the gateway, so they failed only because checkout requests held all the connections.",
        "source_ids": [
          "S08",
          "S06",
          "S17",
          "S15"
        ],
        "alternative": "none"
      },
      {
        "id": "F06",
        "claim": "Checkout 5xx rose from 0 at 09:19:45 to 8% at 09:20:00 (alert) and peaked at ~71% at 09:21:00. p95 latency went from ~215ms to 42s. Both were back to baseline by 09:21:45. The pool-timeout 503s ended at 09:20:59, inside the gateway error window (which ended 09:21:09). So there is no checkout 503 tail after the gateway recovered, and the outage duration was bounded by the gateway incident, with the retry/hold amplification on top of it.",
        "source_ids": [
          "S11",
          "S09",
          "S02",
          "S04",
          "S16",
          "S17"
        ],
        "alternative": "none"
      },
      {
        "id": "F07",
        "claim": "Host CPU saturation ruled out: cpu_percent maxed at 36% across the whole 09:13\u201309:24:30 window (26\u201335% during the incident), far below saturation. The pool-timeout errors show connections were held, not that the CPU was starved.",
        "source_ids": [
          "S05",
          "S10"
        ],
        "alternative": "cpu_saturation"
      },
      {
        "id": "F08",
        "claim": "inventory-service warning ruled out: the only inventory WARN in the window is at 09:20:30, 'SKU HC-2291 below reorder threshold (4 remaining)'. This is a business stock notice that came 50s after errors began. inventory-service logged no ERRORs (only payment-gateway and checkout-api did), the stock check is async per the architecture diagram, and no failing trace contains an inventory span.",
        "source_ids": [
          "S12",
          "S13",
          "S14"
        ],
        "alternative": "inventory_warning"
      },
      {
        "id": "F09",
        "claim": "web-frontend warning ruled out: its only WARN is at 09:20:10, a deprecated legacyTrack() analytics snippet, 'No functional impact'. web-frontend logged no ERRORs, and the 503s are generated inside checkout-api (pool timeout) or come from the gateway, not the frontend.",
        "source_ids": [
          "S12",
          "S13"
        ],
        "alternative": "frontend_warning"
      }
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      {
        "id": "H01",
        "hypothesis": "ROOT CAUSE: dep-8841's retry config (503 retryable, POST allowed, zero backoff, up to 4 attempts) combined with the design of holding a DB connection for the whole checkout, including gateway retries. Together they turned a ~90s third-party gateway 503 burst (TRIGGER) into exhaustion of checkout-api's 15-connection DB pool. FAILURE MODE: customers got generic 503s (and 10\u201342s latency) on POST /checkout and also on GET /cart and /orders/{id}, mostly from 10s pool-checkout timeouts inside checkout-api, plus some gateway-unavailable 503s after 4 attempts.",
        "confidence": "high",
        "supporting_evidence": "The diff shows the retry change. Traces show 4 immediate POST charge attempts holding a connection for 27\u201331s. The pool sat pinned at 15/15 for exactly the gateway window. 105 pool-timeout 503s include 68 on endpoints that never call the gateway. Traffic was healthy between the deploy and the gateway errors. CPU, frontend and inventory were ruled out.",
        "source_ids": [
          "S01",
          "S14",
          "S15",
          "S16",
          "S17",
          "S08",
          "S19",
          "S05"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "H02",
        "hypothesis": "Competing explanation: the gateway outage alone explains the incident, and the retry change only slightly worsened it. This is weakened because most 503s (105 of ~124 checkout-api errors) were pool timeouts, 68 of them on non-gateway endpoints, and under the old config (no POST retries) each connection hold would have been bounded by a single charge attempt. The evidence does not refute it outright: the pool 503s did not outlast the gateway window, and single slow 503 attempts (~7s) might still stress the pool. A counterfactual replay with the old retry config should settle it.",
        "confidence": "low",
        "supporting_evidence": "Gateway errors from 09:19:37 to 09:21:09 bracket the whole checkout error window. Single gateway attempts took 7\u201311s even before any retry.",
        "source_ids": [
          "S16",
          "S17",
          "S14",
          "S13"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "documentation": [
      {
        "id": "D01",
        "url": "https://urllib3.readthedocs.io/en/stable/reference/urllib3.util.html",
        "claim": "urllib3 Retry: a status_forcelist retry happens only if the request method is in allowed_methods and the status is in status_forcelist; with backoff_factor 0 (the default) the sleep between retries returns immediately. This describes documented library behavior, not incident evidence."
      }
    ],
    "alternatives_examined": [
      "cpu_saturation",
      "frontend_warning",
      "inventory_warning"
    ]
